────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Reading choices and scope (the map `data/lean-scope-b/G5.md` §3, l.222–373; coverage `coverage.md`
I-123 … I-128). This is a genuine PDE/dynamics "project"; the discipline is KL16's: formalise what
closes cleanly, and carry every genuine gap as a NAMED HYPOTHESIS, never a `sorry`/`admit`/axiom.

* **Dependencies, leaves-first.** `import`ed and reused, never rebuilt: **KL24** (Lemma 3,
  δJ/δw=−∂_k Π_km, the engine of (ii) and (iii)), which itself brings **KL21** (the Closure Transfer
  Lemma) and **KL22** and the shared `EulerLagrange`; **KL6** (§4.6 phason flatness: a uniform shift
  has no gradient, used in (ii)); **KL7** (arg min_w J = E_⊥, the uniform-equilibria set of (ii)).
  KL23's two dissipation rates (Γa, Γb) enter her proof of (i) as the reason the phason sector can be
  differentiated on its own; here that decoupling is the shape of the model (the phason flow is its
  own gradient flow), recorded in the header, not re-derived.

* **The TWO seams, kept apart deliberately (the architecture skill: one adapter per concept).**
  Part (i) is a statement about the *energy functional* and its gradient flow — its natural home is a
  real Hilbert space `F` (= L²(Ω,E_⊥)), where `δJ/δw` is the gradient and `∫_Ω|δJ/δw|²` is `‖·‖²`.
  Parts (ii)–(iii) are statements about the *spatial field* and its boundary/mean — their home is
  KL24's one-variable reduction, where `δJ/δw=−∂_x Π` is the gradient-only divergence. The map (G5
  §3.1) assigns these two different tool-sets (inner product / `integral_eq_zero_iff` for (i); the
  divergence theorem for (iii)); the module follows, with `grad`/`δJ/δw` the bridge.

* **§1 — part (i), the Lyapunov descent, PROVED (abstract Hilbert gradient flow).** For a gradient
  flow `w'(t)=−Γ_⊥·∇J(w t)` on a real inner-product space, `lyapunov_rate` is her identity
  dJ/dt=−Γ_⊥‖∇J‖² exactly (the chain rule; `‖∇J‖²` is her `∫_Ω|δJ/δw|²`); `lyapunov_nonpos` is ≤0;
  `lyapunov_eq_zero_iff` is her *strict* clause "with equality if and only if δJ/δw≡0".
  `trajectory_unique` is "the trajectory from any initial datum is unique", proved from Mathlib's
  ODE uniqueness with the vector field's **Lipschitz constant carried as the hypothesis** — this is
  exactly the well-posedness the Note marks "standard" (Note L891; Mathlib has no parabolic
  well-posedness theory — G5 §3.1 NOT FOUND), supplied as a named input, never asserted.

* **§2 — part (ii), every uniform phason is an exact equilibrium, PROVED (rides KL24 + KL6).** A
  uniform phason has no gradient (`KL6.uniform_shift_grad_zero`), so Π carries no field term and is
  spatially constant; then by KL24 (`gradient_only_divergence`) δJ/δw=−∂_x Π=0, so the flow velocity
  −Γ_⊥·δJ/δw is 0 — `uniform_equilibrium`. `uniform_equilibria_fill_Eperp` reuses `KL7.isMinOn_free`
  for "the set of uniform equilibria is **all of E_⊥**".

* **§3 — part (iii), the mean phason is an exact constant of the motion, PROVED (the divergence
  core) + one carried regularity step.** `flux_integral_zero` is the genuinely closeable heart: the
  integral of the flux divergence over the sample is the boundary term `Π(b)−Π(a)`, which **vanishes
  under either of her boundary conditions** (periodic: `Π a=Π b`; free/zero-stress: `Π a=Π b=0`) — by
  the fundamental theorem of calculus (`intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt`, the
  one-variable form of her `∮_∂Ω Π_km n_k dS`). `mean_conserved` then assembles her
  d/dt∫w=Γ_⊥∫∂_xΠ=0 ⟹ w̄ constant: the single step **carried** as a named hypothesis is
  `hmean_rate` — that d/dt of the mean equals `Γ_⊥·(flux integral)` — which is her "d/dt commutes
  with ∫ (H7)" and "δJ/δw=−∂_kΠ (Lemma 3)" i.e. differentiation under the integral sign for the
  stated solution. "Given a solution with the stated regularity" (G5 §3.4) is precisely this
  hypothesis; the flux-vanishing it multiplies is proved.

* **§4 — part (iv), the flow relaxes to the initial mean, the FRONTIER (named hypothesis).** The
  decay rate `≥ Γ_⊥ K_min q_min² > 0` rests on a Poincaré/spectral-gap inequality for the elastic
